HOW TO RUN THIS DISK====================THE ARRANGEMENTThis is a data disk: nothing on it boots.  Give it a drive of its own,named both /dd and /h0 -- most programs read their data from /dd, a fewcarry /h0 inside them -- and put your own OS-9 on /h1.  Then    bash /dd/SYS/loginsets PATH, HOME, TERM and TERMCAP, puts /h1/CMDS on the path, and loadsfrom there the utilities programs here run by name (del, copy, tmode,pd and the rest; SYS/login lists them and who wants each).  Without /h1those loads find nothing and the collection still runs; /h1 adds yourOS-9, it is not required.  Your /h1 is also where runb (the BASIC09programs), r68, l68 and cc come from.Getting it there: write the image, osk-freeware.dd, whole onto a diskyour system mounts.  We have not done this on hardware ourselves.  If youhave, what worked is worth writing down here.UNDER os9exec, a community emulator for macOS, Linux and Windows, the samethree devices are environment variables, the image named twice:    OS9DISK=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  OS9H0=<...>/osk-freeware.dd  \    OS9H1=<your OS-9>  os9exec -r bash /dd/SYS/loginUse the image, not a copy unpacked into a directory: permissions andrecord locking work only on the image.  Keep it writable -- scores, savesand logs live on it.  os9exec's `mount -k' makes a blank image for workof your own.The emulator has commands of its own, typed at any prompt inside it, thatanswer what no program here can:    iprocs   processes          imdir    loaded modules    idevs    devices            ipaths   open paths    imem     memory             icopy, imakdir, move, rename`os9exec -ih' lists them.  Lower case: `MDIR' looks for a program.TERMINAL SIZE: 80 BY 24OS-9 cannot ask how big your terminal is, and SYS/termcap has one entry,80 columns by 24 lines, under the names xterm-256color, xterm and vt100.Most full-screen programs believe it whatever your window is.  Taller isharmless; shorter or narrower is not -- `life' writes its status linestraight through its board.  Size the window to 80x24, or edit co# andli# in SYS/termcap to match yours.less, and anything else that reads LINES and COLUMNS, follows the realsize: SYS/login runs `resize', which asks the terminal.  Type `resize'again after changing the window.TERMCAP AS THE ENTRY ITSELFSYS/login sets TERMCAP to the termcap FILE, which nearly every programwants.  sc, gnuchess, gnuchessn, typefast and yahtzee were built againsta library that wants the ENTRY there instead, and stop with    'vt100': Unknown terminal typeAt the bash prompt SYS/login gives you, /dd/.bashrc wraps those five sothey start by name.  Elsewhere, source the entry first, in that shell only:    . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry    gnuchessSetting it for good breaks the others.A MODULE TO LOAD FIRSTThe RTF Fortran system -- rtf, for, lnk, biory, creadoc -- links themodule os9lib.  Without it they print nothing and exit:    load /dd/CMDS/os9libDOC/README-FORTRAN has more.  DOC/STATUS covers the programs for theAtari `Graph' library and `VMod_trap'.YOUR OWN UTILITIES, FORKED BY NAMEA few programs run one of your OS-9's utilities partway through theirwork.  With /h1 mounted they find it; without, they start and then quietlydo nothing:  todos, toos9   del and rename, to put the converted text over the file.                 Without them it waits as todos.___.<n>; `mv' finishes.  creadoc        dir and del, through a shell  uuxqt          procs  rayshade       a shell and cccp in its data directory                 (DOC/rayshade/README-RAYSHADE)Programs that fork a shell are DOC/README-SHELLS.A RAM DISK AT /r0OS-9's C library puts temporary files on /r0, and these carry that path:  bash  ed  hexed  hist  inform  me  pri  preset  rndir  roff  NEWS/rn  UUCP/mail  ELM/filter  GAMES/apfel  GAMES/graphsave  NETPBM/picttoppmA real system has /r0.  Under os9exec, make one first:    mount -r=256k /r0Without it, ed and hexed fail at once and the rest when they reach for atemporary file: `/r0/ed.000003: 000:221 module not found'.PATHS INSIDE THE PROGRAMSSome carry /h0/sys/termcap; all of those read TERMCAP first, so SYS/logincovers them.  A few want data of their own under /h0, the gcc passes andthe linker among them.  Many more read from /dd -- advent's/dd/GAMES/adv/glorkz, fortune's /dd/GAMES/FORTUNE/fortunes.dat, nroff's/dd/LIB/tmac.*.  DOC/DEPENDS lists every path each program opens.BASH, SH AND KSH  - Started bare, bash has no PATH, HOME or TERM.  SYS/login sets them,    and HOME is what makes bash read /dd/.bashrc, where its own `cd' and    `pwd' are replaced: bash's `pwd' hangs on OS-9.  - enable, fc, for, help, if and trap are programs in CMDS and also bash    words, so bash answers first.  Give the path -- /dd/CMDS/help -- and    they run.  - sh cannot fork a command by absolute path.  ksh can be the first    shell (`os9exec -r /dd/CMDS/ksh') but reads no login script.TAKING PART OF ITTo copy a few programs onto your own disk, use `keep' (DOC/README-KEEP):it takes each with every file DOC/DEPENDS lists for it.  Many need morethan their own binary -- a dictionary, a data tree under GAMES.